# Ghostfolio (`ghostfolio-ghostfolio`)

Ghostfolio is an open source, web-based wealth management app for tracking stocks, ETFs, and cryptocurrencies across multiple accounts. It lets you create and manage transactions, import and export portfolio data, and view performance over common time ranges (including dividends/total return). You also get charts, static analysis to spot potential portfolio risks, a mobile-first PWA experience, and a public API for automation (including endpoints for health checks, import/export, and an experimental public portfolio view).

Running Ghostfolio yourself means operating a full stack (NestJS backend, Angular frontend, PostgreSQL, and Redis), managing environment variables and secrets, applying database migrations on upgrades, and keeping the service available for continuous operation. With Ghostfolio on Eyevinn Open Source Cloud (OSC), you can provision a ready-to-use managed instance without setting up servers, databases, or caching infrastructure, and consume it as part of your broader OSC environment instead of maintaining Docker Compose and upgrade routines on your own.

[Source](https://github.com/ghostfolio/ghostfolio)

## Example

```terraform
resource "osc_instance" "example" {
  service_id = "ghostfolio-ghostfolio"
  name       = "myghostfolio"

  parameters = {
    RedisHost = "..."
    # RedisPort = "..."
  }

  sensitive_parameters = {
    DatabaseUrl = var.database_url
    RedisPassword = var.redis_password
    AccessTokenSalt = var.access_token_salt
    JwtSecretKey = var.jwt_secret_key
  }
}
```

Commented lines are optional parameters. Values marked `"..."` need a real value. Outputs are `url`, `external_ip`, `external_port` and the raw `instance` document, see the `osc_instance` resource.

## Parameters

| Name | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `DatabaseUrl` | string | yes | PostgreSQL connection URL for Ghostfolio, for example postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname. The schema is migrated and seeded automatically on every start. The OSC entrypoint (fork only) adds connect_timeout=300 if the URL does not set it. Feeds DATABASE_URL. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `RedisHost` | string | yes | Host name of the Redis or Valkey server Ghostfolio uses, for example the internal cluster name of an OSC Valkey instance. Feeds REDIS_HOST. |
| `RedisPort` | string | no | Port of the Redis or Valkey server. Defaults to 6379. Feeds REDIS_PORT. |
| `RedisPassword` | string | no | Password of the Redis or Valkey server (for OSC Valkey, its Password option). Leave empty if the server has no password. Feeds REDIS_PASSWORD. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `AccessTokenSalt` | string | yes | Random string used to derive users' security tokens. It must stay the same across restarts: if it changes, existing security tokens stop working and users are locked out. Choose a long random value and keep it safe. Feeds ACCESS_TOKEN_SALT. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `JwtSecretKey` | string | yes | Random string used to sign login tokens (JWT). Choose a long random value and keep it secret. Feeds JWT_SECRET_KEY. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |

The instance `name` must match `^\w+$` and be unique per service within the workspace. OSC lowercases it and allows at most 20 characters.

Parameter values are strings; booleans take `"true"` or `"false"`. Reference an `osc_secret` as `osc_secret.<name>.ref` to keep a value out of the instance configuration. This page is generated from the OSC catalog; the provider validates against the live catalog when planning.
